# Armor Attack sound board -- netlist converted from MAME
#
# Cinematronics vector games carry a small audio board of their own, driven from the
# CPU board's output latch: the latch bits arrive here as ports (OUT_0 ... OUT_7, MAME's
# TTL_INPUT names), and the board's sound goes out to a final amplifier that MAME does
# not simulate and this file therefore does not contain.
#
# Source: MAME mame0289 (commit f34f02505e32), src/mame/cinematronics/nl_armora.cpp,
# NETLIST_START(armora). Licence CC0-1.0 by the file's own header (line 1),
# copyright-holders Aaron Giles (line 2).
# https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mamedev/mame/mame0289/src/mame/cinematronics/nl_armora.cpp
# Converted by tools/mame_netlist.py; checked by tools/build_board_nets.py.
#
# What the conversion means. MAME's netlists are written to be simulated, so this is a
# second reading of the sheet by someone else, not a read of it: designators and pin
# numbers are MAME's unless the notes say the sheet was read to correct them. Names on
# `label` lines are MAME's, which mostly follow the sheet's with Q for an overbar; `net`
# is used only for the supply rails. A `port` is a net that leaves what this file
# describes -- a signal from the CPU or the other board, a cabinet control, or a source
# MAME simulates with a stand-in rather than the circuit.
#
#   net NAME: PIN ...     a supply rail
#   label NAME: PIN ...   a net MAME names
#   wire PIN PIN ...      an unnamed connection
#   port NAME: PIN ...    a net that leaves this file (see above)
#   nc PIN ...            a pin with nothing attached
#   parallel PIN ...      outputs the board sums on one node by design
#   value REF VALUE       a passive's value, in the unit MAME's own macro uses
#
# PIN is DESIGNATOR.NUMBER. Diode pin 1 is the cathode; transistors keep B, C and E;
# MAME does not record which way round an electrolytic goes, so C pins are MAME's 1/2.
# The sheet numbers its ICs IC1, IC2 ...; they are written U1, U2 ... here, because the
# checker files every IC under a U designator.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Undone: what MAME does to simulate the board that the board does not do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# line 27: #define HLE_TANK_VCO 1 -> 0 (high-level stand-in for a real circuit)
# line 654: NET_C(GND, IC7.1, IC9.4, IC9.5, IC9.12, IC9.13, IC27.1, IC27.2) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Unconnected inputs" block
# line 655: NET_C(GND, IC24.2, IC24.3) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Unconnected inputs" block
# Declarations MAME comments out but the restored circuit uses, restored: Q1 (line 228)
# Stand-ins that are not parts (the nets they drive are ports): TTL_INPUT(I_OUT_0), TTL_INPUT(I_OUT_1), TTL_INPUT(I_OUT_2), TTL_INPUT(I_OUT_3), TTL_INPUT(I_OUT_4), TTL_INPUT(I_OUT_7)
#
# Left out by MAME (commented out at TAG, so not in this file either):
#   line 85: RES(R24, RES_K(390))  -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 86: RES(R25, RES_K(390))  -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 145: RES(R84, RES_K(15))   -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 146: RES(R85, 150)         -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 147: RES(R86, RES_K(22))   -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 148: RES(R87, 150)         -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 149: RES(R88, 0.51)        -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 150: RES(R89, 0.51)        -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 154: POT(R93, RES_K(10))   -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 173: CAP(C1, CAP_U(0.1))   -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 174: CAP(C2, CAP_U(22))    -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 175: CAP(C3, CAP_U(0.1))   -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 176: CAP(C4, CAP_U(22))    -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 177: CAP(C5, CAP_U(22))    -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 178: CAP(C6, CAP_U(0.1))   -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 179: CAP(C7, CAP_U(22))    -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 180: CAP(C8, CAP_U(0.1))   -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 181: CAP(C9, CAP_U(22))    -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 182: CAP(C10, CAP_U(0.1))  -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 200: CAP(C28, CAP_U())   -- don't see it anywhere
#   line 210: CAP(C38, CAP_U(0.68))  -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 211: CAP(C39, CAP_P(470))   -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 212: CAP(C40, CAP_P(470))   -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 213: CAP(C41, CAP_U(0.005)) -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 214: CAP(C42, CAP_P(470))   -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 218: D_1N4003(D1)        -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 219: D_1N4003(D2)        -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 220: D_1N4003(D3)        -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 221: D_1N4003(D4)        -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 223: D_1N4003(D6)        -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 224: D_1N4003(D7)        -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 228: Q_2N3904(Q1)            // NPN -- not used
#   line 239: Q_2N6292(Q12)           // NPN -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 240: Q_2N6107(Q13)           // PNP -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 260: TTL_7815_DIP(IC4)       // +15V Regulator -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#   line 261: TTL_7915_DIP(IC5)       // -15V Regulator -- part of voltage converter (not emulated)
#
# MAME's file says 'Entire schematic needs a verification pass'. With HLE_TANK_VCO off the
# tread VCO is MAME's own description of the board's circuit; it uses Q1, which MAME
# declares only in a comment ('not used' -- by the stand-in), so the declaration is
# restored.
#
# Not compared with the sheet. MAME's designators, values and pin numbers are taken as
# MAME has them; the checker shows the netlist is internally consistent (every pin exists,
# no two outputs fight, nothing floats), which is not the same as matching the board.

